/**
\page choosing Choosing a sampler

All five samplers are used the same way: construct, call `define(...)`, then call
`operator()` once per sample. Each owns a seedable `std::mt19937`, and each accepts your own
engine instead.

\section choosing_table Which sampler

| To load                                                    | Use                                | Minimal call |
| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------ |
| An anisotropic population with power-law tails             | `bi_kappa_distribution`            | `d.define(kappa, theta_perp, theta_par, ub)` |
| An anisotropic Maxwellian population                       | `bi_maxwellian_distribution`       | `d.define(theta_perp, theta_par, ub)` |
| Speeds from your own density, isotropic direction          | `general_velocity_generator`       | `g.define(gW, w_min, w_max)` |
| Parallel speeds from your own density, along the field     | `field_aligned_velocity_generator` | `g.define(gW, w_min, w_max, theta_perp, ub, +1)` |
| Positions from your own spatial density                    | `general_position_generator`       | `g.define(dim, lower, upper, rho)` |

`bi_kappa_distribution` and `bi_maxwellian_distribution` transform standard random variates
directly. The other three use rejection sampling against an envelope estimated in
`define(...)`; see \ref params_rejection.

\section choosing_velocity Velocity samplers compared

| | `bi_kappa` | `bi_maxwellian` | `general_velocity` | `field_aligned_velocity` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Tails            | power law, set by `kappa`   | Gaussian            | set by `g(w)` | set by `g(w)` (parallel); Gaussian (perpendicular) |
| Anisotropy       | `theta_perp`, `theta_par`   | `theta_perp`, `theta_par` | none (isotropic) | `g(w)` parallel, Maxwellian perpendicular |
| Uses `ub`        | yes                         | yes                 | no            | yes |
| Method           | direct transformation       | direct transformation | rejection   | rejection (parallel part) |
| Velocity cap     | 20 thermal speeds by default; `no_cap()` removes it | 20 thermal speeds by default; `no_cap()` removes it | none; `w` is limited to `[w_min, w_max]` | perpendicular components, 20 `theta_perp` by default; `no_cap()` removes it |
| Returns          | `std::array<Real,3>`        | `std::array<Real,3>` | `std::array<Real,3>` | `std::array<Real,3>` |

With the default cap, `bi_kappa_distribution` samples the bi-Kappa distribution conditioned on
a box of 20 thermal speeds per component. For `kappa <= 3/2` this removes an appreciable part of
the tail. Pass `no_cap()` to sample the bi-Kappa distribution itself; see \ref params_cap.

\section choosing_units Units

The samplers do not fix units. Velocities are returned in the units of `theta_perp`,
`theta_par` or `sqrt(w)`, and positions in the units of the bounds. No mass enters: for a
distribution written in energy, use `w = |v|^2 = 2E/m`.

\section choosing_next See also

- \ref parameters lists every argument, default and valid range.
- \ref usage gives examples, including seeding and a shared engine.
- \ref usage_bikappa_cap describes what the velocity cap does.
*/
